      "textEn": "236\nFrom the fifth canto of Shrimad Bhagwat, we can know the distance of the various worlds scattered in the immense material sky.\nWe can. Beyond the world we live in, there are countless universes, and all of these together make up that\nspiritual sky, which is called the eternal Brahmaloka above, a negligible fraction. In the following verse (8.16) of Bhagavad Gita, the wise ones kindly call back their abode. Aabrambhovanolloka: Punarbaratinoou Juni. There is no rebirth, O son of Kunti. The supreme abode of the universe, Satyaloka, which is situated below the eternal Brahmaloka, and its description\nis given above, and all other worlds are material. In these physical worlds, a person's place is now\nstill subject to the laws of nature, namely birth, death, old age, and disease. But when a man enters the eternal sky of the eternal Brahmaloka,\nwhich is the abode of God, then he gets complete liberation from the aforementioned material sufferings.\nTherefore, liberation, as imagined by thoughtful philosophers (knowers) and yogis, is only\nobtained when one becomes a devotee of God. He who is not a devotee of God, he cannot enter the abode of God.\nHe cannot be admitted. Only by attaining service in a spiritual state can a person enter the abode of God. Therefore, philosophers and yogis should first be attracted to the Bhakti-Sampradaya,\nonly then can they truly attain liberation. The earth is imagined, the sky is imagined, the earth is imagined, the sky is imagined.\nThe self-world is imagined, the head is imagined, thus is the world imagination. 42). Meaning of the word\n\nBhurloka: the entire world from the netherworld to the earth; Kalyitah-- based on imagination; Padabhyam-- situated on the feet;\nBhuvarloka-- Bhuvarloka; Asya-- of the complete form of God; Nabhitah-- from the navel; Swarloka-- starting from the heavenly realm;\nUrdhvam-- the upper world; Kalpitah-- imagined; Murdhna-- from the chest to the head; Iti-- thus; Va-- or else; Loka--\nLoka; Kalpa-- imagination; Kalpa--\nOther people can divide the entire worlds into three parts. Their names are--Underworld (up to the earth), Middleworld (between the earth), and head from the chest to the head"
