      "textEn": "245\nPayu:--bowel; Yamasya:--the regulator of death; Mritasya:--of the friend; Parimokhsastaya:--of the anus; Narada:--hey Narada; Himsaya:--of violence; Nirakrteh:--of the unrighteous; Mrityu:--of death; Nirayasya:--of hell; Gudam:--bowel; Smritah:--is understood..\n\nOh Narad, the anus of the immense form of God is the abode of the god of death, Mitra, and\nthe end of the large intestine of God is considered the place of envy, misfortune, death, hell, etc. Parabhuteradharmascha tamaschaapi paschimah. Nadya nad-nadinam cha gotramansthi- sanhatihi. 10>. Meaning of the word:\n\nParabhuteh--of anxiety; Adharmasya--of unrighteousness; Tamasah--of ignorance; Cha--and; Api--also; Paschimah--western; Naghyah--of nerves; Nad--of large rivers; Nadinam--of streams; Cha--and; Gotranam--of mountains; Asthi--of bones; Sankhitih--of\nCompilation L. Rivers and streams flow from their veins, and huge mountains have been formed on their bones.\nMeaning: To refute the formless concept of the complete Purushottama Bhagwan, here\na systematic analysis of the structure of the divine body of God is given. The description of the body of the Lord (Virat) is in such a way that it is clear that the Lord's form is different from the normal worldly concept. In any\nstate, they are not formless. Since ignorance is the back part (backside) of God, therefore, ignorance of the ignorant\nIgnorance is also not separate from the physical conception of God. Because their body is the aggregate form of every object,\ntherefore, no one can claim that they are only without attributes. In contrast,\nit is proven by the complete description of God that He is both with form and without form. Of God\nThe tangible form is their original form, and their unparalleled light is merely a reflection of their divine body.\nThose who get the opportunity to see God from the front, only they can experience the tangible form of God.\nBut those who are deprived and therefore keep the ignorant aspect of God aside,\nor those who see God from behind, they can only experience the unmanifest form of God."
