      "textEn": "246\nOf the unmanifest-rasa-sindhunaam bhutanam niyyathasya cha. The stomach is known, the mind is the measure. 11). Shabdartham\n\nAvyakta nirvishesha swaroop; Ras-sindhunaam -of the ocean of juice; Bhutanam -of those born in the physical world; Nirvanasya -of destruction; Cha -also; Udaraam -his stomach; Viditam -known; Ya -by; Pumsah -by male; Mahaapurusha's -of the great man; Hridayam -heart)\nManasah -of the subtle body; Padam -place, Pad..\n\nThe formless form of God is the abode of oceans, and their womb is destroyed from a physical point of view.\nIt is the refuge of living beings. Their heart is the abode of the subtle physical bodies of living beings. Intelligent people know it\nIn this way. Meaning: The Bhagavad Gita (8.17-18) states that according to human calculation, Brahma's\none day is equal to one thousand yugas of all four yugas (each 420,000 years), and Brahma's night\nis also as long. Brahma lives for a hundred years, and then he dies.\nBrahma, who is generally regarded as a great devotee of the god, attains liberation only at his death. In this\nway, the universe (a football-like spherical body controlled by Brahmaji) is destroyed, and\nresidents of a particular realm or the entire universe are also destroyed. In this verse, the word 'Avyakta' means the night of Brahma, when there is a partial destruction, and the beings of that particular universe,\neven up to Brahmaloka, including large oceans, take refuge in the belly of the Virat Purusha. When the night of Brahma passes, creation happens again, and all the beings residing in the belly of God are released to play their respective roles, as if they have awakened from deep sleep. Because living beings are never destroyed,\ntherefore, when the physical world is destroyed, the existence of living beings is not erased, but until liberation\nis attained, the living being has to abandon one body and again and again assume another body.\nThis human life is to find a solution to the repeated changes of the body, as a result of which,\nas a result, one can get a place in Chidakash, where every object is of the form of Sachchidananda. In other words,\nthe subtle forms of living beings reside in the heart of the Supreme Being, and at the time of creation, they take form."
